          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "A box contains different coloured counters, with",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "break"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "P(\\text{purple})=10\\%",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ", ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "P(\\text{yellow})=0.\\dot{6}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": " and ",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "latex": "P(\\text{orange})=\\frac{1}{7}",
              "display": false
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": ".",
              "marks": []
            },
            {
              "type": "break"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Is it possible to obtain a colour other than purple, yellow or orange? If so, state the probability.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "Yes; the probability of another colour is $\\frac{19}{210}$.",
      "worked": "$0.\\dot{6}=\\frac{2}{3}$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(\\text{other colour})&=1-\\left(\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{2}{3}+\\frac{1}{7}\\right)\\\\P(\\text{other colour})&=1-\\frac{21+140+30}{210}\\\\P(\\text{other colour})&=1-\\frac{191}{210}\\\\P(\\text{other colour})&=\\frac{19}{210}\\end{align*}$$\nYes, because the remaining probability is positive.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 22
  },
